    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full build passes. Here's what I did to satisfy the spec while keeping structural impact low:\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added two read-only fields to the `OwnerDto` schema: `possibleDuplicate` (boolean, default false) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (int32). MapStruct's generated mapper leaves these unmapped, same as the other derived fields (`namesakeCount`, etc.).\n\n2. **`PossibleDuplicate.java`** (new) \u2014 a small cohesive helper (modeled on the existing `Namesakes`/`Household` helpers) with one static method: finds the earliest existing owner sharing this owner's last name (case-insensitive) and postcode but with a *different* telephone, returning its id or `null`. Requiring a different telephone means a hard `IdentityKey` duplicate (which would already have been rejected at create) never counts, and a blank postcode short-circuits to no match.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** and **`RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 two lines each to call the helper and set the flags on the DTO.\n\n## Why this keeps structural cost down\n\nThe whole detection algorithm lives in a **new class with no sibling methods** (`WMC_other = 0`), so it pays nothing for surrounding context. Each responder edit is a small addition to a single-method class (also `WMC_other = 0`), and I avoided touching any large class (no changes to `Owner`, the mapper, or the pipeline YAML). The behaviour is added as small, self-contained units rather than by growing an existing method.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
